    <item>
      <title>GLANCE: Global Actions in a Nutshell for Counterfactual Explainability</title>
      <link>https://dsachar.net/publication/2026-aaai-kpt/</link>
      <pubDate>Thu, 01 Jan 2026 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate>
      <guid>https://dsachar.net/publication/2026-aaai-kpt/</guid>
      <description>&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;p&gt;We propose a method for global explainability of black box models using counterfactual explanations. A counterfactual explanation locally explains an outcome by providing the minimal changes necessary to reverse the outcome, e.g., &amp;ldquo;if you had five more years of experience, your job application would have been accepted&amp;rdquo;. We develop a method, termed GLANCE, that summarizes all counterfactual explanations for a given model.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;















&lt;figure  id=&#34;figure-three-actions-that-summarize-all-counterfactual-explanations&#34;&gt;
  &lt;div class=&#34;d-flex justify-content-center&#34;&gt;
    &lt;div class=&#34;w-100&#34; &gt;&lt;img alt=&#34;image&#34; srcset=&#34;
               /publication/2026-aaai-kpt/GLANCE_hu10903146438223607317.webp 400w,
               /publication/2026-aaai-kpt/GLANCE_hu17815352061748901114.webp 760w,
               /publication/2026-aaai-kpt/GLANCE_hu3463568111219529215.webp 1200w&#34;
               src=&#34;https://dsachar.net/publication/2026-aaai-kpt/GLANCE_hu10903146438223607317.webp&#34;
               width=&#34;760&#34;
               height=&#34;119&#34;
               loading=&#34;lazy&#34; data-zoomable /&gt;&lt;/div&gt;
  &lt;/div&gt;&lt;figcaption&gt;
      &lt;em&gt;Three actions that summarize all counterfactual explanations.&lt;/em&gt;
    &lt;/figcaption&gt;&lt;/figure&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Solving this global version of counterfactual explainability is different than finding the local counterfatual explanations and picking among them.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;















&lt;figure  id=&#34;figure-a-toy-example-depicting-two-negative-instancesx1-x2-and-five-actions-a-the-feature-space-the-line-is-thedecision-boundary-b-the-action-space-l1-l2-depict-the-de-cision-boundary-from-the-perspective-of-x1-x2-respectively&#34;&gt;
  &lt;div class=&#34;d-flex justify-content-center&#34;&gt;
    &lt;div class=&#34;w-100&#34; &gt;&lt;img alt=&#34;image&#34; srcset=&#34;
               /publication/2026-aaai-kpt/example_hu1589924553338578423.webp 400w,
               /publication/2026-aaai-kpt/example_hu9797865795064969311.webp 760w,
               /publication/2026-aaai-kpt/example_hu15232130839713007938.webp 1200w&#34;
               src=&#34;https://dsachar.net/publication/2026-aaai-kpt/example_hu1589924553338578423.webp&#34;
               width=&#34;760&#34;
               height=&#34;440&#34;
               loading=&#34;lazy&#34; data-zoomable /&gt;&lt;/div&gt;
  &lt;/div&gt;&lt;figcaption&gt;
      &lt;em&gt;A toy example depicting two negative instances
x1, x2, and five actions. (a) The feature space; the line is the
decision boundary. (b) The action space; l1, l2 depict the de-
cision boundary from the perspective of x1, x2, respectively.&lt;/em&gt;
    &lt;/figcaption&gt;&lt;/figure&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;
</description>
